Chlorophyll-Crush

Creative Catalyst: Botanists in the Amazon discovered that when subjected to high-voltage interference, rainforest flora emit frantic, rhythmic electrical discharges that mimic the structure of 1960s disco percussion.

This genre is a violent, hyper-kinetic collision of 1960s dancefloor hedonism and raw, botanical rage. It sounds like a thousand glass bells shattering in a hurricane, grounded by the thick, rubbery thump of a distorted, walking disco bassline. The "vocals" are often manipulated bio-feedback signals, sounding like organic matter screaming through a 1960s plate reverb, layered with abrasive, metallic chimes that feel like shards of wet glass cutting through the humid jungle heat.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 124–138 BPM, characterized by a "gasping" syncopated disco beat that periodically stops dead, mimicking a plant losing electrical charge.
*   Harmonic Palette: Diminished-major clusters inspired by the Fibonacci sequence found in leaf patterns; heavy reliance on dissonant minor seconds that resolve into shimmering, beat-matched bell tones.
*   Production Signatures: "Photosynthetic Saturation" (pushing tape gain until it produces a leafy, crackling top end), "Stoma-Gating" (noise gates synced to the electrical bio-rhythm of a fern), and "Gong-Filtering" (routing the entire master bus through a 6-foot suspended glass gong).
*   Dynamics: Start-stop brutality; sudden, deafening swells of crystalline resonance that snap back into dry, claustrophobic funk.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement, known as the "Horticultural Riot," began in 1968 inside a subterranean botanical conservatory in Manaus. Founded by renegade bio-acousticians who sought to liberate the "captive intelligence" of the Amazon, the scene revolves around nocturnal "Root-Raves." Participants dress in translucent, bioluminescent PVC ponchos and heavy iron jewelry, performing rituals where they plug 1960s modular synth racks into strangler figs to modulate the music via sap-pressure spikes. It is an aesthetic of sweaty, high-fashion biology—a rebellion against the artificial, prioritizing the "aggressively living" over the static machine.

Typical Instruments: A 1960s Farfisa organ modified to trigger massive Soaring Shival Bells, hand-forged Glass Gongs amplified via piezoelectric mics, fuzz-drenched Hofner basses, and bio-modulated Moog oscillators.

For Fans Of: The Silver Apples, Liquid Liquid, Lee "Scratch" Perry (the jungle years), and Annea Lockwood.

